:"Draw the direction towards each attracting mass and measure distances from the centres of the masses.","notes":["Outside a spherical mass, g = GM/r² and F = mg. Add field contributions as vectors when more than one mass is present.","An altitude above a surface is not the radius in the formula: r = R + h. The test mass affects force but not the field strength at its position."]},"fep-0f9d4ecaf6e949d5-q12:whole":{"hint":"For a circular orbit, gravity supplies the inward acceleration. Use the radius measured from the central body’s centre.","notes":["Equating GMm/r² = mv²/r gives v = √(GM/r), then T = 2πr/v. This leads to T² proportional to r³.","Orbiting objects are in free fall, not outside gravity. A geostationary orbit must also be equatorial, prograde and have Earth’s rotation period."]},"fep-1f1fe4bacfda04af-q14:whole":{"hint":"Draw the direction towards each attracting mass and measure distances from the centres of the masses.","notes":["Outside a spherical mass, g = GM/r² and F = mg. Add field contributions as vectors when more than one mass is present.","An altitude above a surface is not the radius in the formula: r = R + h. The test mass affects force but not the field strength at its position."]},"duplicate-5ed02a916efec8f2":{"hint":"Draw the direction towards each attracting mass and measure distances from the centres of the masses.","notes":["Outside a spherical mass, g = GM/r² and F = mg. Add field contributions as vectors when more than one mass is present.","An altitude above a surface is not the radius in the formula: r = R + h. The test mass affects force but not the field strength at its position.","Link the relevant physical cause to the observation asked about. Naming a law alone does not explain why this particular result follows."]},"fep-4702f1ae6785e44e-q11:whole":{"hint":"For a circular orbit, gravity supplies the inward acceleration. Use the radius measured from the central body’s centre.","notes":["Equating GMm/r² = mv²/r gives v = √(GM/r), then T = 2πr/v. This leads to T² proportional to r³.","Orbiting objects are in free fall, not outside gravity. A geostationary orbit must also be equatorial, prograde and have Earth’s rotation period."]},"cross-ed24u5-6:whole":{"hint":"Use the inverse-square force and the qualitative consequence of Kepler’s second law.","notes":["Greater distance from the Sun means weaker gravitational force. A planet moves more slowly on the distant part of its ellipse, consistent with equal areas swept in equal times.","Do not substitute an instantaneous distance into the circular-orbit speed formula for this elliptical orbit."],"method":"orbit","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-aq24p2-21:whole":{"hint":"For a circular orbit, gravity supplies the inward acceleration. Use the radius measured from the central body’s centre.","notes":["Equating GMm/r² = mv²/r gives v = √(GM/r), then T = 2πr/v. This leads to T² proportional to r³.","Orbiting objects are in free fall, not outside gravity. A geostationary orbit must also be equatorial, prograde and have Earth’s rotation period."],"method":"orbit","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-aq24p2-22:whole":{"hint":"Compare the starting total mechanical energy with the minimum needed to reach infinity.","notes":["With zero potential at infinity, the minimum escape condition is ½mv_e² − GMm/R = 0, so v_e = √(2GM/R).","Escape speed is different from circular orbital speed at the same radius. For ratios, use √(M/R); the escaping object’s mass cancels."],"method":"escape","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-aq24p2-23:whole":{"hint":"Distinguish energy per kilogram from the energy of the whole object.","notes":["With zero at infinity, gravitational potential is V_g = −GM/r and potential energy is mV_g. Work required for a slow move is m(V_final − V_initial).","Potential is a scalar, so add its contributions algebraically. Use mgΔh only for a small height change where g is approximately uniform."],"method":"gravity-potential","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-oc24p1-14:whole":{"hint":"Compare the starting total mechanical energy with the minimum needed to reach infinity.","notes":["With zero potential at infinity, the minimum escape condition is ½mv_e² − GMm/R = 0, so v_e = √(2GM/R).","Escape speed is different from circular orbital speed at the same radius. For ratios, use √(M/R); the escaping object’s mass cancels."],"method":"escape","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ca24m4-written-1a:whole":{"hint":"Start by stating where the zero of potential is chosen.","notes":["Potential is energy per unit mass. The negative sign depends on the zero at infinity and the attractive nature of gravity; it does not mean a negative mass or a repulsive force."],"method":"gravity-potential","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ca24m4-written-1biii:whole":{"hint":"Include the kinetic energy the satellite already has in orbit.","notes":["Minimum escape corresponds to zero speed at infinity. Using GMm/r alone overestimates the extra energy because the orbiting satellite already has kinetic energy."],"method":"escape","review_status":"reviewed"}}},"answers":{"entries":{"fep-872b290ac78a68f4-qG3:whole":{"status":"editorial-solution","text":"Newton’s second law gives mi a = mg g, hence a = (mg/mi)g. Equal free-fall accelerations for different materials, with air resistance absent, show that the ratio of gravitational mass to inertial mass is the same for all bodies. Choosing consistent units makes these masses equal.","display_text":"On the Moon, a hammer and a feather are released from rest at the same height and at the same time. Both reach the surface at the same time.\n\nExplain how this demonstrates the equivalence of gravitational mass and inertial mass.","correction":"Removed the source’s mistaken historical attribution and retained the physical experiment and assessed task."}}},"presentation":{"practice/parts/fep-6b5c443f4c508e3a-q10-whole-c233e1f36b011bfc.webp":{"crop":[0,6,1051,339],"size":[1051,401],"masks":[[14,5,33,35]]